      {
        "id": "email-18-0",
        "type": "email",
        "date": "2026-03-13",
        "summary": "Topical Map = 3X more SEO traffic",
        "subject": "Topical Map = 3X more SEO traffic",
        "details": "If you've been publishing content that is not ranking on Google,\r\nI have a story for you.\r\n\r\nWhen I started ranking my first website back in 2009, I\r\nunknowingly used a simple topical map.\r\n\r\nAnd so I learned SEO THAT IS THE WAY to get good results.\r\n\r\nBut let me share a real case study from our client portfolio.\r\n\r\nWe had a client in the AI Video SaaS space who was completely\r\ndependent on paid influencers for traffic.\r\n\r\nThey asked if I could help them with SEO to diversify\r\ndistribution.\r\n\r\nThey did have a looot of content, but it wasn't ranking.\r\n\r\n(proof below, but read first!)\r\n\r\nSo we started working:\r\n\r\nBuilt their Topical Map\r\n\r\nWe created a structured content hierarchy that organized all\r\ntheir topics into logical clusters.\r\n\r\nArranged their Content Clusters\r\n\r\nWe identified which pages should link to each other, creating\r\nclear pathways for both users and Google to follow.\r\n\r\nLaunched a 50-article sprint\r\n\r\nMy team wrote these articles based on the topical map (I always\r\npush for a substantial body of work at the beginning of an SEO\r\ncampaign).\r\n\r\nRe-organized their existing content\r\n\r\nWe rearranged their older articles to fit into the new structure\r\nwe\u2019d discovered through the Topical Map.\r\n\r\nWhat made this approach special wasn\u2019t just the content creation\r\n\u2013 it was the SYSTEM.\r\n\r\nSEO can be chaotic, but having a clear content architecture makes\r\neverything more manageable.\r\n\r\nTheir team learned to use these assets themselves, which proved\r\nto be the most valuable part.\r\n\r\nThe results?\r\n\r\nThey tripled their daily traffic, and the last time I checked,\r\nalmost 80% of the visitors were coming from SEO \u2013 a significant\r\nshift from their previous 95% dependence on paid influencers.\r\n\r\nThis isn\u2019t an isolated case.\r\n\r\n* A SaaS startup grew from 3K visits/month to 120K visits/month\r\nin 5 months\r\n* Another increased their traffic from 300K to 1MM clicks in 6\r\nmonths\r\n\r\nAll thanks to proper content architecture.\r\n\r\nWe didn't do much new content.\r\n\r\nJust organized w",
        "direction": "inbound",
        "createdAt": "2026-03-16T12:18:59"
      },
      {
        "id": "email-18-1",
        "type": "email",
        "date": "2026-03-11",
        "summary": "Winning Startups Build Systems",
        "subject": "Winning Startups Build Systems",
        "details": "I started learning SEO in 2008.\r\n\r\nAfter my first $100K year, I started teaching it too.\r\n\r\nSome students completely changed their lives with my Udemy\r\ncourse: building SEO businesses, making anywhere from $50K to\r\n$250K a year, and in some cases eventually exiting for six\r\nfigures.\r\n\r\nBut one thing kept bothering me:\r\n\r\nA lot of people study SEO. Far fewer apply it long enough to\r\nreally benefit from it.\r\n\r\nLater, when I started working with startups, I noticed the same\r\npattern again.\r\n\r\nThe teams that won were not always the ones with the best theory\r\nor the cleanest best practices at the start.\r\n\r\nThey were the ones that kept going, experimented more, and\r\nimproved faster as they learned.\r\n\r\nWhen strong systems got layered on top of that, things could move\r\nvery quickly.\r\n\r\nTaplio was one early example in my agency.\r\n\r\nWe were early in experimenting with AI, executing free tools and\r\nlarge programmatic SEO campaigns.\r\n\r\nBy month 5, that had grown to 120K visits per month.\r\n\r\nWe kept refining from there, and the business later exited for\r\n$8M.\r\n\r\nSince then, I\u2019ve been trying to repeat that kind of outcome with\r\nthe startups we work with.\r\n\r\nAnd the biggest gap I keep seeing now is this:\r\n\r\nManual SEO is (still) expensive. Most AI setups are messy.\r\n\r\nAnd most teams do not need more knowledge or disconnected tools.\r\n\r\nThey need a system that actually gets used.\r\n\r\nSo I\u2019m packaging things differently now.\r\n\r\nI\u2019m offering a managed AI + SEO system for WordPress (initially).\r\n\r\nThe goal is to give teams a more scalable way to run content and\r\nSEO without hiring more people or duct-taping tools together.\r\n\r\n(This is one simple example (\r\nhttps://1f74bdb9.click.kit-mail3.com/8kumn6v3nzaoh2d5zp5akhkg2qx73i3hzq76q/dpheh0hevx50v5am/aHR0cHM6Ly9wMzguZjIubjAuY2RuLnppZ2h0LmNvbS9pdGVtcy9nZXVHWU5ZMS85YTI0OTA5NC02NmZiLTQ4MDAtYmVkZC1kYzgxNWM2NmRhY2UuanBnP3NvdXJjZT12aWV3ZXImdj05Mjg3MjMyYjk2MDViMjMzNDZlZWIzY2ZkZTczMzllZg==\r\n) of the things it can do, adding missing queries to a met",
        "direction": "inbound",
        "createdAt": "2026-03-16T12:18:59"
      },
      {
        "id": "email-18-2",
        "type": "email",
        "date": "2026-03-06",
        "summary": "The Number That Matters is Hidden",
        "subject": "The Number That Matters is Hidden",
        "details": "S. knows my philosophy better than most: content structure over\r\neverything.\r\n\r\nWe've worked together on 3 different businesses.\r\n\r\nBut when he launched his new SaaS, he skipped the structure.\r\n\r\nClassic founder move.\r\n\r\nSo I stepped mid-2025, and we did a few moves to kick things into\r\ngear.\r\n\r\nThe graph is impressive, but the number that really matters is\r\nthe average position: 27.1 \u2192 10.3\r\n\r\nIt matters because getting into the top 10 is what really moves\r\nthe needle.\r\n\r\nHow we turned things around\r\n\r\nThere was content published, and the content was being found, but\r\nnot trusted enough to rank where it mattered.\r\n\r\nFor two reasons: structure, but also coverage.\r\n\r\nLet me explain: the site had a poor content organization and that\r\nneed to be solved.\r\n\r\nBut also, the content didn't have enough topical coverage.\r\n\r\nSo we needed to fix both.\r\n\r\nThe first thing: to analyze the architecture of the site and then\r\nfixing it.\r\n\r\nThe usual suspects showed up:\r\n\r\n* weak topical authority\r\n* no cluster structure\r\n* pages competing\r\n\r\nThe work:\r\n\r\n* cleaned up cannibalization\r\n* rebuilt the topical map\r\n* connected the clusters\r\n\r\nFor Topical Coverage we needed to cover more about the theme of\r\nthe SaaS they run.\r\n\r\nSo we added it up in the Topical Map.\r\n\r\nTip > If you write about \"email marketing\" but never touch\r\ndeliverability, list segmentation, or open rates, Google sees\r\ngaps. And gaps signal a site that doesn't fully own the topic.\r\n\r\nThe new content was covering all the angles needed to support the\r\nnew structure.\r\n\r\nWith these two moves, 6 months later, we've got here:\r\n\r\n* Clicks: 3.16K \u2192 12.8K\r\n* Impressions: 497K \u2192 2.32M\r\n* Average position: 27.1 \u2192 10.3\r\n\r\nThe growth is never guaranteed, but it's a lot more probable if\r\nyou craft it since the beginning.\r\n\r\nStructure tells Google what your site is about.\r\n\r\nCoverage tells Google you actually know the topic.\r\n\r\nMost sites are missing both.\r\n\r\nIf you want to know what yours is missing, I have something cool:\r\n\r\nI buil",
        "direction": "inbound",
        "createdAt": "2026-03-16T12:18:59"
      },
      {
        "id": "email-18-3",
        "type": "email",
        "date": "2026-03-05",
        "summary": "A Topical Map that netted me over $1MM",
        "subject": "A Topical Map that netted me over $1MM",
        "details": "When I started ranking my first website back in 2009\u2026 I\r\nunknowingly used a simple topical map.\r\n\r\nI then hired a team to do it for me.\r\n\r\nBack then I was using only Google Docs, nothing fancy.\r\n\r\nToday, I'll share it what I did with you \u2193\r\n\r\nWhat you need for a Topical Map\r\n\r\n* Picking the right articles to write\r\n* Planning the content (Pillar Pages // Content Clusters with\r\nexample)\r\n* Writing the Content\r\n* Publishing\r\n* Monitoring\r\n* Scaling\r\n\r\nI'm amazed that this simple setting allowed me get around 8MM\r\nvisitors/year and over 1MM in the last 4-5 years.\r\n\r\nLet's get to it!\r\n\r\n\u200b\r\n\r\n1. Picking the right articles to write\r\n\r\nExamine closely related posts or articles and consider the\r\nfollowing questions:\r\n\r\n- Do these articles remain relevant to each other?\r\n\r\n- Do they complement each other effectively?\r\n\r\nIf you find that the answer to these questions is affirmative,\r\ncontinue reading.\r\n\r\nSOURCE: SEMRUSH.COM\r\n\u200b\r\n\r\n2. Planning the Content (Topical Maps)\r\n\r\nCreate Pillar Pages (Main Categories)\r\n\r\nFrom here, link to your secondary pages and link them all\r\ntogether follow the image above (taken from SEMRUSH, and SEO\r\nsoftware)\r\n\r\nAlso, below is a photo of how I did it in the past, without any\r\npaid software or anything, just open a doc and start Googling\r\nuntil you build your full topical map.\r\n\r\n\u200b\r\n2.1 AI Vs. The Past\r\n\r\nIn the past, you needed 1-5 days to think about all these stuff,\r\nresearch and put it together.\r\n\r\nWith AI you don't need this anymore, I have an AI topical map\r\ntool (\r\nhttps://1f74bdb9.click.kit-mail3.com/p9u70w530df9h262567sqhp08e60vtrhkgnxg/dpheh0hev9mogmsm/aHR0cHM6Ly9odG1sZHJvcHMuY29tL3RvcGljYWwtbWFw\r\n) that can give you what you need in about 5 minutes.\r\n\r\n\u200b\r\n\r\n3. Writing the content\r\n\r\nAssign a deadline to each of your content pieces.\r\n\r\nStick to it yourself or ask your writer to do so.\r\n\r\n(or your AI Agent)\r\n\r\nSounds stupid, but really, put a deadline to everything, and\r\ndoesn't matter rain or shine, make sure you deliver.\r\n\r\nIf you do i",
        "direction": "inbound",
        "createdAt": "2026-03-16T12:18:59"
      }
